RDGA SEVEN INDETERMINATE FORMS 
CARRIER / Γ VERSION
(Clashes of carriers and recomputation, not real limits)

----------------------------------------------------------------
RDGA OBJECTS
    R          : relation
    Φ          : carrier map, Φ : R ↦ I_R
    I_R        : carrier ideal in ℝ[x₁,…,xₙ]
    Γ          : recomputation step (update carriers, re-solve, set Q flags)
    𝒮(R)       : real zero set of I_R
    dim(I_R)   : Krull dimension of ℝ[x]/I_R
    dim_exp(R) : expected dimension from the Q layer

RDGA CUES
    "→ 0"        : a generator is redundant, meaning f lies in the ideal
                   generated by the others (division or Gröbner reduction),
                   or a side-constraint (A ≠ B, r > 0) becomes equality.
                   Evaluation f(x₀) = 0 is not by itself redundance.

    "unbounded"  : elimination fails to cut a fiber; dim(I_R) stays strictly
                   above dim_exp(R).

    "resolved"   : reduced ideal, admissible nonempty 𝒮(R), Q_OK.

    "indeterminate" : the next Γ step returns the same ideal and the same rank.

Q FLAGS
    Q_OK        : dim(I_R) = dim_exp(R), 𝒮(R) nonempty, rank sufficient
    Q_EMPTY     : no real zero
    Q_DEGEN     : dimension mismatch after a successful reduction
    Q_SINGULAR  : Jacobian rank deficient; generators not separated
    Q_UNCHECKED : not yet evaluated

----------------------------------------------------------------
FORM 1: 0 / 0 → collapse–collapse of generators (BOT)

TYPE:
    BOT

VARS:
    f ∈ I_R, g ∈ I_S both vanish on the current state.
    I_R + I_S does not decide which generator remains.

FUNDAMENTAL:
    Vanishing at a point is not enough:
        test ideal membership of each generator modulo the others.

RESOLUTION:
    • One elimination or one Jacobian step:
        - Gradients independent → 0-dimensional intersection,
          Q_OK or a discrete set.
        - Both gradients vanish, and one further elimination does not
          separate the generators → BOT persists, Q_SINGULAR.
    • Side-constraint reaches equality (A = B, r = 0):
        presentation degeneracy.
        - Zero set empty → Q_EMPTY.
        - Zero set nonempty but dimension wrong → Q_DEGEN.

EXAMPLE:
    Line–circle incidence with r = 0:
        radius side-constraint has failed.
        Distinct parallel lines forced to intersect → Q_EMPTY.
        Coincident lines → Q_DEGEN (dimension 1 vs expected point).

----------------------------------------------------------------
FORM 2: ∞ / ∞ → unresolved fibers (Div/Div)

TYPE:
    Div/Div (typed by which projection failed)

VARS:
    Both projections π(R), π(S) fail to cut their fiber:
        eliminants leave dim(I_R), dim(I_S) > dim_exp.

FUNDAMENTAL:
    Not typed by numeric excess; dimension size does not decide.

RESOLUTION:
    • Form Elim(I_R + I_S):
        - If eliminant lowers dim to dim_exp and 𝒮 ≠ ∅:
              that eliminant is next carrier, Q_OK.
        - If elimination does not lower dim:
              unresolved fiber; no dominance by "larger excess".
    • Equal failure on both projections:
        eliminate one further variable and reclassify remainder.
        If that also fails, Γ has not separated them.

----------------------------------------------------------------
FORM 3: 0 · ∞ → mixed composition (MIX)

TYPE:
    MIX

VARS:
    One factor imposes a vanishing constraint;
    the other leaves an intermediate variable free:

        R ∘ S = Elim_y(I_R + I_S),

    with y constrained in one ideal and absent from the other.

FUNDAMENTAL:
    MIX asks whether elimination removes y or retains a free factor.

RESOLUTION:
    • Vanishing generator kills free fiber → reduced ideal.
    • Intermediate variable retained → higher-dimensional carrier,
      MIX persists.

NOTE:
    This is RDGA’s only "product":
        composition by elimination, not scalar multiplication.

----------------------------------------------------------------
FORM 4: ∞ − ∞ → generator cancellation under ideal sum (Div−Div)

TYPE:
    Div−Div (cancellation or retention)

VARS:
    Both ideals contain a leading constraint f,
    and f reduces to zero modulo remaining generators of I_R + I_S.

FUNDAMENTAL:
    Cancellation is ideal membership, not numeric subtraction.

RESOLUTION:
    • If f ∈ (remaining generators):
        drop f, recompute remainder ideal, compare dim with dim_exp.
    • If f not redundant and other generators do not cancel it:
        sum keeps f; that relation sets next carrier.

NOTE:
    Do not use parallel lines here:
        distinct parallels → unit ideal, 𝒮 = ∅, Q_EMPTY.
        coincident lines → side-constraint collapse, Form 1’s degeneracy.

----------------------------------------------------------------
FORMS 5–7: 1^∞, ∞^0, 0^0 → no RDGA operator

TYPE:
    none (unless defect already inside a carrier)

VARS:
    RDGA operators:
        union, intersection, negation, projection, composition.
    Carriers:
        polynomial ideals.

FUNDAMENTAL:
    Do not add a power operator.

ADMISSION:
    • Only when a carrier polynomial already contains the defect:
        - genuine exponential subexpression, or
        - transform defect inside Y − T(X).
      Then classify that defect by Forms 1–4.
    • Otherwise drop the form.
      Transform composition T₂ ∘ T₁ is already Elim of Map carriers.

----------------------------------------------------------------
DECISION RULE (ONE Γ STEP)

Given an operator (sum, eliminant, composition):

    • Elim(I_R + I_S) has dim = dim_exp and 𝒮 ≠ ∅:
          resolved, Q_OK.

    • Elimination does not lower dimension:
          unresolved fiber, typed by projection that failed.
          No numeric dominance.

    • A generator reduces to zero modulo the others:
          drop it and reclassify (cancellation branch).

    • Jacobian rank drops and one further elimination does not
      separate generators:
          BOT, Q_SINGULAR.

    • Composition retains intermediate variable:
          MIX.

    • Real zero set empty:
          Q_EMPTY.

    • Dimension differs from expected after successful reduction:
          Q_DEGEN.

BOT and MIX:
    flags, not solution sets.
    Either is permanent only if next Γ step returns same ideal
    and same rank.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY

    0/0        → BOT:
        generators vanish and are not separated;
        redundance is ideal membership.

    ∞/∞        → Div/Div:
        elimination fails to cut the fiber;
        no excess-dimension winner.

    0·∞        → MIX:
        vanishing constraint against a free intermediate variable.

    ∞−∞        → Div−Div:
        shared generator reduces to zero and is dropped.

    1^∞, ∞^0, 0^0:
        only if already inside a carrier; otherwise dropped.

    The clash is an ideal-sum or elimination tie.
    The answer is the next carrier, its real zero set, or a Q flag.

----------------------------------------------------------------
APPENDIX — DIVISION CALCULUS BESIDE CARRIER CALCULUS

A.1 CROSS-MODE QUOTIENT

    Quotient is sort-valued, not ideal-valued.
    Numerator provenance: SID.
    Denominator provenance: PED, passed through inversion.

    N / D = N ∘_{S,I} ι(D)

    Reductions:

        • D inverts to non-identity Z_I, and N = SID(a) not structural zero:
              EXP(SID(a))  (expansion fiber).
              Structural degrees of freedom retained.
              EXP ≠ BOT.

        • N is structural zero Z_S, and D inverts to non-identity PED(a):
              COL(ι(PED(a)))  (collapse fiber).
              No structural degrees of freedom.
              COL ≠ BOT.

        • Both sides zero:
              Z_S ∘_{S,I} Z_I = BOT  (clash sort).
              Absorbing as argument.
              Not a locus, not a point of 𝒮(R).

    Refused rewrites:
        EXP(X) = BOT,
        COL(J) = BOT,
        b/b as a unit.
        Repeated fiber with its inverse is not a unit unless
        a unit is defined separately (none here).

A.2 WHAT MUST NOT BE IDENTIFIED

    Division sorts are not Q flags:

        EXP(SID(a))              ≠ Form 2 (failed elimination)
        COL(ι(PED(a)))           ≠ Q_EMPTY or Q_DEGEN
        BOT (sort)               ≠ reduced ideal, discrete point,
                                   or either fiber

    Failed elimination:
        remains unresolved fiber, typed by projection that failed.

    Empty real zero set:
        remains Q_EMPTY.

    Dimension mismatch after reduction:
        remains Q_DEGEN.

    Rank failure not separated by further elimination:
        remains Q_SINGULAR.

A.3 SHARED BOT

    Record both provenances only when both present:

        BOT = ( Z_S ∘_{S,I} Z_I , rank-deficient sum )

    Sort half:
        division calculus panel (Z_S ∘ Z_I).

    Ideal half:
        Form 1:
            two generators vanish,
            ideal membership does not drop either,
            gradients dependent,
            further elimination does not separate.

    If only sort present:
        do not invent Jacobian.
    If only ideal test present:
        do not invent Z_S, Z_I.

    BOT is a flag, not element of 𝒮(R).
    Persists only when next Γ step returns same ideal and same rank.

A.4 DECISION ORDER

    On a Γ step, classify expression before choosing calculus:

        • Cross-mode quotient:
              apply A.1.
              Result: EXP, COL, or BOT.
              Stop. No dimension test.

        • Ideal sum, projection, relational composition:
              apply main decision rule.
              Result: reduced ideal, MIX, unresolved fiber, or Q flag.
              Stop. No EXP or COL.

        • 1^∞, ∞^0, 0^0:
              drop, unless carrier polynomial already contains defect;
              then classify defect by Forms 1–4.

    Form 2:
        still "elimination did not cut the fiber," not expansion.

    Form 4:
        still "generator reduced to zero modulo others," not collapse.

    Line–circle with r = 0, distinct parallels, coincident lines:
        stay on Q flags assigned in Form 1.

A.5 STATUS

    • Body:
        closed under RDGA operators (sum, elimination, projection,
        composition, Q flags).

    • Appendix:
        closed under quotient reductions (EXP, COL, BOT).

    • Neither half derives the other.
      Neither is marked confirmed by the other.

    • Quotient-stability:
        the refusal in A.2, not a separate theorem.
